      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy, didn't I? Sorry about that. Let's get back to the task at hand.\n\nTo investigate the calibration of the sensors, you can try the following:\n\n1. Check the sensor's calibration history: Review the sensor's maintenance records to see if it has been calibrated recently. If it hasn't, it's possible that the sensor is not accurately counting the stock.\n2. Verify the sensor's unit of measure: Ensure that the sensor is set to count individual units, not cases. You can do this by checking the sensor's settings or consulting the manufacturer's documentation.\n3. Test the sensor's accuracy: Perform a manual count of a small batch of stock to verify the sensor's accuracy. This will give you a baseline to compare against the sensor's readings.\n4. Check for sensor placement: Ensure that the sensor is placed in a location that is not obstructed by other objects or personnel. A clear line of sight to the stock is essential for accurate counting.\n\nAs for human error, you can try the following:\n\n1. Review inventory management procedures: Ensure that all staff members are following the same procedures for counting and recording inventory.\n2. Train staff on inventory management: Provide",
